{"abstract":[{"text":"Understand the architectural patterns that make Swidux work correctly with SwiftUI’s observation system.","type":"text"}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/swidux\/architectureguide"]}],"sections":[],"seeAlsoSections":[{"generated":true,"title":"Explanation","identifiers":["doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PluginArchitecture","doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/DesignPrinciples","doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SecurityPosture"],"anchor":"Explanation"}],"kind":"article","identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/ArchitectureGuide","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux"]]},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Swidux relies on specific patterns to ensure correct "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"type":"text","text":" behavior, efficient rendering, and safe concurrency. This article explains the reasoning behind these patterns."}]},{"text":"The Snapshot Pattern","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"The-Snapshot-Pattern"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Store.send()"},{"type":"text","text":" copies the observer tree into a local struct, mutates the copy via the reducer, then assigns changed properties back. This happens internally via "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"SwiduxObservable"},{"type":"text","text":" — "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State(observer:)"},{"type":"text","text":" packs, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"State.apply(_:to:)"},{"type":"text","text":" unpacks."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The pattern exists for two reasons:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","items":[{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" equality checking.","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"type":"text","text":" The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"set"},{"type":"text","text":" accessor checks "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Equatable"},{"type":"text","text":" and suppresses no-op notifications. Swift’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"_modify"},{"type":"text","text":" accessor (used by "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"inout"},{"type":"text","text":") fires notifications unconditionally. The snapshot pattern routes through "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"set"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Cross-slice observation isolation.","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"type":"text","text":" The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Swidux"},{"type":"text","text":" macro generates a separate "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Observable"},{"type":"text","text":" class for each "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Slice"},{"type":"text","text":" property. A view reading "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"store.items"},{"type":"text","text":" won’t re-render when only "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"store.ui"},{"type":"text","text":" changes, because they live on different observer objects."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"@Swidux"},{"type":"text","text":" macro generates the observer class tree and "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"SwiduxObservable"},{"type":"text","text":" conformance automatically. Hand-written conformance is still possible for advanced cases — see "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SwiduxObservable","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"style":"note","type":"aside","name":"Note","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Explicit equality guards (","type":"text"},{"code":"if x != state.x { x = state.x }","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") are unnecessary. ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" already checks equality on ","type":"text"},{"code":"set","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". Unconditional assignment is safe.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"text":"Dispatch Loop Detection","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"Dispatch-Loop-Detection"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PersistencePlugin","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" warns if "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"afterReduce"},{"type":"text","text":" is called more than 100 times per debounce interval. This usually means "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"send()"},{"type":"text","text":" isn’t using the snapshot pattern, causing cascading re-renders that trigger re-dispatches."}]},{"text":"Reducer Weight","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"Reducer-Weight"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Reducers run synchronously on the MainActor. Move O(n²) work into an "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/Effect","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" and dispatch the result back as an action."}]},{"text":"Effect Threading","type":"heading","level":2,"anchor":"Effect-Threading"},{"style":"important","type":"aside","name":"Important","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Effects run on Swift concurrency’s cooperative thread pool via ","type":"text"},{"code":"Task { @concurrent in }","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". Blocking calls (","type":"text"},{"code":"Process.waitUntilExit()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"DispatchSemaphore.wait()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"Thread.sleep()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") hold threads hostage. If enough block, the pool starves and the MainActor freezes.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Use async alternatives: "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"terminationHandler"},{"type":"text","text":" + continuation instead of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"waitUntilExit()"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task.sleep()"},{"type":"text","text":" instead of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Thread.sleep()"},{"type":"text","text":", async file I\/O instead of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Data(contentsOf:)"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]}],"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"Swidux"}],"role":"article","title":"Architecture & Performance","roleHeading":"Article"},"references":{"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/DesignPrinciples":{"kind":"article","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/designprinciples","role":"article","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The 10 rules that shape every Swidux app, and the reasoning behind them."}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/DesignPrinciples","title":"Design Principles"},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux","role":"collection","abstract":[{"text":"Redux-style state management for SwiftUI, with macros for observability and ready-made plugins for persistence, undo, paywalls, version killswitches, and parental gates.","type":"text"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/swidux","title":"Swidux"},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/SecurityPosture":{"role":"collectionGroup","title":"Security Posture","kind":"article","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/SecurityPosture","url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/securityposture","abstract":[{"text":"What Swidux’s soft controls actually guarantee, and what to do when you need a hard one.","type":"text"}]},"doc://Swidux/documentation/Swidux/PluginArchitecture":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/Swidux\/documentation\/Swidux\/PluginArchitecture","role":"article","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Extend the dispatch cycle with lifecycle hooks — from invisible infrastructure to domain-specific features."}],"type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/swidux\/pluginarchi